        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(opencode): public models work without configured credentials",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenCode Zen has an official credentialless-client contract for public models: when no account credential is configured, the OpenCode client sends the non-secret API key `public` and disables models that have any positive input-cost tier. OpenClaw instead rejected those eligible models at auth preflight with `No API key found for provider \"opencode\"`.\n\nAuthoritative upstream contract:\n\n- OpenCode source at commit [`b8142c7`](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/b8142c7aa8f88222873fb79d636e312e28037c2d/packages/core/src/plugin/provider/opencode.ts#L164-L176) assigns `apiKey = \"public\"` without credentials and disables models when any input-cost tier is positive.\n- OpenCode's own tests cover [`uses a public key and disables paid models without credentials`](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/b8142c7aa8f88222873fb79d636e312e28037c2d/packages/core/test/plugin/provider-opencode.test.ts#L202-L225), [`keeps free models without credentials`](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/b8142c7aa8f88222873fb79d636e312e28037c2d/packages/core/test/plugin/provider-opencode.test.ts#L228-L251), and output-only-cost eligibility.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe OpenCode plugin now follows that upstream contract directly:\n\n- returns the non-secret `public` API key only for catalog models whose base and tiered input costs are all zero;\n- derives eligibility from the existing Zen catalog cost metadata instead of a hard-coded model-ID allowlist;\n- uses the normal authenticated OpenAI-compatible request path, with no Authorization-header removal or image-path bypass;\n- threads the selected model ID through the generic provider synthetic-auth seam so model listing, availability, and execution agree;\n- keeps paid and unknown models credential-gated.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers can run OpenCode Zen models that the official OpenCode contract classifies as public without configuring a personal key. Paid or unknown OpenCode models still fail closed when no credential is present.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Red: the contract regression expected the manifest and provider resolver to use `public`; the previous implementation returned its private `no-auth` marker.\n- Focused green:\n  - `src/agents/model-auth.test.ts`: 81 passed.\n  - `extensions/opencode/index.test.ts`: 16 passed, including catalog-wide cost/auth alignment.\n  - affected core/status/gateway suites: 5 files, 160 passed.\n  - formatter and focused non-type-aware Oxlint checks passed.\n- Final-head third-party live proof on commit `089d9cc1486`, using Node 24.15.0, isolated state, and both OpenCode key variables unset:\n  - `openclaw agent --local --model opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free ...`\n  - real `POST https://opencode.ai/zen/v1/chat/completions`;\n  - HTTP 200 `text/event-stream` in 1826 ms;\n  - exact visible response `PUBLIC_PROBE_BOUNDED_OK`;\n  - one successful OpenCode attempt, no fallback, exit 0.\n- Paid control on the same build and isolated environment:\n  - `opencode/gpt-5.5` failed with `ProviderAuthError: No API key found for provider \"opencode\"`;\n  - no provider transport started; expected exit 1.\n- CI follow-up: the first new-head run exposed three timeout regressions because model-scoped auth availability could probe the full plugin runtime for unrelated providers, including prepared direct-auth and models-list paths. The follow-up declares `opencode` as a synthetic-auth candidate and gates runtime probes on positive runtime/manifest metadata. The three formerly failing files now pass locally: 15/15, 23/23, and 13/13; the focused auth/availability, synthetic-auth runtime, and OpenCode suites pass 116/116, 9/9, and 16/16 respectively.\n\nThe repository type-aware lint wrapper is locally blocked before the target files by existing `packages/net-policy/src/ip.ts`/`ipaddr.js` type errors; this PR does not modify that package. Targeted lint and affected tests above are green.",
